# Postmortem: EC2 Disk Exhaustion Incident
**Date:** July 2026  
**Severity:** P1 — Critical  
**Duration:** ~2 hours  
**Author:** Chukwuebuka Emmanuel Orjide  

---

## Summary
EC2 instance disk reached 100% capacity causing 
k3s Kubernetes scheduler to stop scheduling pods 
due to disk pressure taint. The root cause was 
k3s downloading container images without sufficient 
disk space available.

---

## Timeline

| Time | Event |
|------|-------|
| T+0 | Disk usage noticed at 100% on login |
| T+10 | kubectl get pods showing pending status |
| T+15 | kubectl describe pod revealed disk-pressure taint |
| T+20 | Identified k3s images consuming 493MB in /var/lib/rancher |
| T+30 | Removed terraform .terraform directory (675MB freed) |
| T+45 | Expanded EBS volume from 8GB to 20GB via AWS Console |
| T+90 | Filesystem expanded with growpart and resize2fs |
| T+120 | Disk at 37% — normal operations resumed |

---

## Root Cause

Two contributing factors:
1. Terraform provider cache (.terraform directory) 
   consuming 675MB unnecessarily
2. k3s container runtime downloading system images 
   (493MB) without pre-checking available disk space

The EC2 instance had only 8GB storage which was 
insufficient for running multiple services simultaneously:
- Jenkins (96MB)
- Docker images
- k3s images
- Terraform cache
- Application code

---

## Impact

- Kubernetes pods unable to schedule for ~2 hours
- Node.js application deployment blocked
- Development workflow interrupted

---

## Resolution

1. Removed .terraform cache directory (freed 675MB)
2. Cleaned snap package cache
3. Expanded EBS volume from 8GB to 20GB
4. Extended filesystem using growpart and resize2fs
5. Disk usage reduced from 100% to 37%

---

## What Went Wrong

- No disk usage monitoring alerts configured
- Insufficient initial disk size for planned workloads
- No cleanup policy for Terraform provider cache

---

## Action Items

| Action | Priority | Status |
|--------|----------|--------|
| Configure Grafana alert for disk > 80% | High | ✅ Done |
| Document disk requirements before installing tools | High | ✅ Done |
| Add .terraform to .gitignore | Medium | ✅ Done |
| Expand EC2 storage proactively | High | ✅ Done |

---

## Lessons Learned

1. Always monitor disk space before installing 
   heavy tools like k3s
2. Terraform provider cache should never be 
   committed or left on production servers
3. 8GB storage is insufficient for a full 
   DevOps toolchain — minimum 20GB recommended
4. Disk pressure monitoring should be configured 
   before problems occur — not after

---

## Prevention

- Grafana alert now configured for disk > 80% ✅
- EC2 storage expanded to 20GB ✅
- Regular disk cleanup procedures documented ✅
